1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,750
There are about 16,000 ETFs in the world.

2
00:00:03,770 --> 00:00:06,920
Together, they hold around 24 trillion dollars.

3
00:00:08,130 --> 00:00:09,490
But almost all of them fit

4
00:00:09,510 --> 00:00:10,780
into one of twelve boxes.

5
00:00:11,970 --> 00:00:12,840
Learn the boxes,

6
00:00:12,860 --> 00:00:14,960
and you'll know exactly what you're buying.

7
00:00:14,980 --> 00:00:16,270
In 11 minutes.

8
00:00:17,530 --> 00:00:19,390
No hype, and no stock picks.

9
00:00:19,410 --> 00:00:21,290
ETFs can lose value.

10
00:00:21,310 --> 00:00:23,780
This is education, not financial advice.

11
00:00:25,070 --> 00:00:26,830
First, what is an ETF?

12
00:00:26,850 --> 00:00:28,760
It's a basket of investments.

13
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:29,600
One share gives you

14
00:00:29,620 --> 00:00:31,830
a small slice of everything inside.

15
00:00:33,130 --> 00:00:36,020
ETF stands for exchange-traded fund.

16
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:36,990
It trades on a stock

17
00:00:37,010 --> 00:00:38,890
exchange like a normal share,

18
00:00:38,910 --> 00:00:39,760
all day long.

19
00:00:40,900 --> 00:00:42,600
Instead of buying hundreds

20
00:00:42,620 --> 00:00:43,510
of shares one by one,

21
00:00:43,530 --> 00:00:45,460
you buy a single ETF,

22
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:47,280
and pay one small yearly fee.

23
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:50,500
In Europe, most ETFs follow

24
00:00:50,520 --> 00:00:53,100
the EU's fund rules, called UCITS.

25
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:53,950
That's why their names

26
00:00:53,970 --> 00:00:55,730
often end with those letters.

27
00:00:57,070 --> 00:00:59,460
UCITS funds keep your investments separate

28
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:01,050
from the company that runs them.

29
00:01:01,070 --> 00:01:02,950
If that company goes bust,

30
00:01:02,970 --> 00:01:05,040
the investments still belong to the fund.

31
00:01:06,530 --> 00:01:09,210
American ETFs like VOO usually don't

32
00:01:09,230 --> 00:01:12,320
publish the EU's key information document.

33
00:01:12,340 --> 00:01:13,580
So most European brokers

34
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:15,080
won't sell them to you.

35
00:01:16,270 --> 00:01:17,930
Let's open the first box.

36
00:01:19,570 --> 00:01:20,310
Box 1.

37
00:01:20,330 --> 00:01:21,660
Index ETFs.

38
00:01:22,170 --> 00:01:23,340
The biggest box.

39
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:24,800
An index ETF simply

40
00:01:24,820 --> 00:01:26,710
copies a list of companies,

41
00:01:26,730 --> 00:01:30,270
like the S&P 500 or the MSCI World.

42
00:01:31,670 --> 00:01:35,430
The MSCI World holds around 1,300 companies,

43
00:01:35,450 --> 00:01:37,480
from 23 developed countries.

44
00:01:39,030 --> 00:01:40,780
But more than two-thirds of it

45
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:41,840
is American companies.

46
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:43,860
The rest is spread across Japan,

47
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:44,900
Europe and others.

48
00:01:46,270 --> 00:01:48,170
Bigger companies get a bigger weight.

49
00:01:48,190 --> 00:01:49,500
When a company grows,

50
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:50,060
its share

51
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:52,380
of the index grows with it, automatically.

52
00:01:53,630 --> 00:01:54,830
Nobody picks the stocks.

53
00:01:54,850 --> 00:01:56,670
A computer follows the list,

54
00:01:56,690 --> 00:01:58,690
which is why the fees can be tiny.

55
00:01:59,970 --> 00:02:02,690
Some S&P 500 ETFs cost as

56
00:02:02,710 --> 00:02:06,030
little as 0.03% a year.

57
00:02:06,050 --> 00:02:10,020
A typical bank fund often charges around 1.8%.

58
00:02:11,470 --> 00:02:13,900
Invest €200 a month for 30

59
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:15,600
years at 7% a year,

60
00:02:15,620 --> 00:02:16,530
and that fee gap

61
00:02:16,550 --> 00:02:19,010
can cost you more than €60,000.

62
00:02:20,770 --> 00:02:23,250
Some index ETFs buy the shares directly.

63
00:02:23,270 --> 00:02:25,260
That's called physical.

64
00:02:25,280 --> 00:02:27,040
Others get the same return through

65
00:02:27,060 --> 00:02:28,470
a contract with a bank.

66
00:02:28,490 --> 00:02:29,990
That's called synthetic.

67
00:02:31,230 --> 00:02:34,870
The first ETF launched in America in 1993.

68
00:02:34,890 --> 00:02:36,470
Europe got its first

69
00:02:36,490 --> 00:02:38,420
ones in 2000, in Frankfurt.

70
00:02:38,440 --> 00:02:40,460
Today, it's a giant industry.

71
00:02:42,330 --> 00:02:43,110
Box 2.

72
00:02:43,130 --> 00:02:44,920
Country and region ETFs.

73
00:02:45,470 --> 00:02:46,630
Some ETFs zoom

74
00:02:46,650 --> 00:02:48,350
in on one part of the world.

75
00:02:48,370 --> 00:02:50,430
Europe, Japan, emerging markets,

76
00:02:50,450 --> 00:02:52,090
or even a single country.

77
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:56,790
Emerging markets, like China, India, Taiwan or Brazil,

78
00:02:56,810 --> 00:02:58,140
can offer more growth,

79
00:02:58,160 --> 00:03:00,280
but also more risk and bigger swings.

80
00:03:01,530 --> 00:03:03,060
And when you buy another country,

81
00:03:03,080 --> 00:03:04,970
you also buy its currency.

82
00:03:04,990 --> 00:03:07,420
A Japan ETF moves with the yen.

83
00:03:08,670 --> 00:03:11,230
Some investors combine a world ETF

84
00:03:11,250 --> 00:03:13,420
with an emerging markets ETF.

85
00:03:13,440 --> 00:03:16,770
Others pick one all-world ETF that holds both.

86
00:03:17,970 --> 00:03:19,290
Many people mostly buy

87
00:03:19,310 --> 00:03:21,110
companies from their own country.

88
00:03:21,130 --> 00:03:23,050
That's called home bias.

89
00:03:23,070 --> 00:03:25,020
But even big European countries are only

90
00:03:25,040 --> 00:03:27,490
a few percent of the world's stock market.

91
00:03:28,830 --> 00:03:31,780
The catch with a single country is concentration.

92
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:35,090
Japan's stock market peaked in 1989,

93
00:03:35,110 --> 00:03:37,420
and took 34 years to get back there.

94
00:03:39,170 --> 00:03:39,980
Box 3.

95
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:41,250
Sector ETFs.

96
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:44,730
Sector ETFs hold just one industry.

97
00:03:44,750 --> 00:03:47,740
Technology, healthcare, banks, energy, and so on.

98
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:49,740
They can soar

99
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:51,540
when their industry is in fashion,

100
00:03:51,560 --> 00:03:52,930
and crash when it isn't.

101
00:03:54,170 --> 00:03:56,710
Energy stocks fell hard in 2020,

102
00:03:56,730 --> 00:03:59,970
then became the best performing sector of 2022.

103
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:03,750
And if you already own a world ETF,

104
00:04:03,770 --> 00:04:05,430
a tech ETF on top means

105
00:04:05,450 --> 00:04:07,770
buying the same big companies twice.

106
00:04:08,900 --> 00:04:11,910
A world ETF already holds every sector.

107
00:04:11,930 --> 00:04:13,450
A sector ETF is a bet

108
00:04:13,470 --> 00:04:14,870
on just one of them.

109
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:17,320
Box 4.

110
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:18,740
Thematic ETFs.

111
00:04:19,570 --> 00:04:21,870
Thematic ETFs follow a story.

112
00:04:21,890 --> 00:04:25,870
Artificial intelligence, robots, clean energy, cybersecurity.

113
00:04:27,230 --> 00:04:28,190
They often launch

114
00:04:28,210 --> 00:04:29,760
when the story is already hot,

115
00:04:29,780 --> 00:04:31,830
and the stocks are already expensive.

116
00:04:33,030 --> 00:04:35,310
Morningstar found that over 15 years,

117
00:04:35,330 --> 00:04:38,800
only about 9% of thematic funds both survived

118
00:04:38,820 --> 00:04:40,180
and beat global stocks.

119
00:04:41,870 --> 00:04:42,670
Many closed,

120
00:04:42,690 --> 00:04:45,260
or lagged behind a simple world ETF,

121
00:04:45,280 --> 00:04:47,320
partly because of higher fees.

122
00:04:47,340 --> 00:04:49,900
A good story isn't always a good investment.

123
00:04:51,770 --> 00:04:52,540
Box 5.

124
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:53,870
Factor ETFs.

125
00:04:54,670 --> 00:04:57,270
Factor ETFs, also called smart beta,

126
00:04:57,290 --> 00:04:58,880
tilt toward one trait.

127
00:04:58,900 --> 00:05:02,220
Cheap stocks, small companies, momentum, or quality.

128
00:05:03,530 --> 00:05:05,130
There's also equal weight.

129
00:05:05,150 --> 00:05:07,450
Every company gets the same slice,

130
00:05:07,470 --> 00:05:09,470
instead of letting the giants dominate.

131
00:05:10,830 --> 00:05:13,480
Academic research found that some of these traits

132
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:15,790
paid a little extra over very long periods.

133
00:05:16,970 --> 00:05:18,620
But they can lag for years.

134
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:20,530
Value stocks trailed growth stocks

135
00:05:20,550 --> 00:05:22,160
for most of the 2010s.

136
00:05:23,430 --> 00:05:24,960
A factor ETF only works if

137
00:05:24,980 --> 00:05:26,130
you have the patience

138
00:05:26,150 --> 00:05:27,910
to hold it through the bad years.

139
00:05:29,670 --> 00:05:30,330
Box 6.

140
00:05:30,350 --> 00:05:31,820
Dividend ETFs.

141
00:05:32,430 --> 00:05:34,800
Dividend ETFs pick companies that pay out

142
00:05:34,820 --> 00:05:36,730
a lot of cash to their shareholders.

143
00:05:37,930 --> 00:05:39,800
But a dividend isn't free money.

144
00:05:39,820 --> 00:05:40,770
When it's paid out,

145
00:05:40,790 --> 00:05:42,010
the share price drops

146
00:05:42,030 --> 00:05:43,430
by about the same amount.

147
00:05:44,630 --> 00:05:45,600
A very high yield

148
00:05:45,620 --> 00:05:47,260
can even be a warning sign.

149
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:48,770
Sometimes the price has fallen

150
00:05:48,790 --> 00:05:50,710
because the business is in trouble.

151
00:05:51,930 --> 00:05:54,980
In Europe, many ETFs come in two versions.

152
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:57,430
Distributing pays the dividends to you.

153
00:05:57,450 --> 00:06:00,080
Accumulating reinvests them automatically.

154
00:06:01,270 --> 00:06:02,810
Which one suits you depends

155
00:06:02,830 --> 00:06:04,380
on how your country taxes them,

156
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:06,020
so check your local rules.

157
00:06:07,870 --> 00:06:08,690
Box 7.

158
00:06:08,710 --> 00:06:09,800
Bond ETFs.

159
00:06:10,570 --> 00:06:12,070
Bond ETFs hold loans,

160
00:06:12,090 --> 00:06:14,180
to governments or to companies.

161
00:06:14,200 --> 00:06:16,400
You earn interest instead of profits.

162
00:06:17,530 --> 00:06:19,790
Government bonds from strong countries

163
00:06:19,810 --> 00:06:21,110
are usually the safest.

164
00:06:21,130 --> 00:06:22,990
Company bonds pay more interest,

165
00:06:23,010 --> 00:06:24,590
because the risk is higher.

166
00:06:25,800 --> 00:06:27,730
They usually move less than stocks,

167
00:06:27,750 --> 00:06:29,820
which is why many people mix the two.

168
00:06:31,130 --> 00:06:32,590
When interest rates go up,

169
00:06:32,610 --> 00:06:33,950
older bonds lose value,

170
00:06:33,970 --> 00:06:35,530
because new bonds pay more.

171
00:06:37,170 --> 00:06:38,340
But not always.

172
00:06:38,360 --> 00:06:41,180
In 2022, interest rates jumped,

173
00:06:41,200 --> 00:06:43,810
and global bonds lost about 16%,

174
00:06:43,830 --> 00:06:45,170
their worst year on record.

175
00:06:46,430 --> 00:06:48,300
Short-term bonds move less.

176
00:06:48,320 --> 00:06:49,970
Long-term bonds move a lot more

177
00:06:49,990 --> 00:06:51,620
when interest rates change.

178
00:06:53,030 --> 00:06:55,810
Bonds in another currency add currency risk.

179
00:06:55,830 --> 00:06:57,750
That's why many bond ETFs come

180
00:06:57,770 --> 00:06:59,530
in a version hedged to euros.

181
00:07:01,270 --> 00:07:01,990
Box 8.

182
00:07:02,010 --> 00:07:03,580
Money market ETFs.

183
00:07:04,200 --> 00:07:06,940
Money market ETFs follow the interest rate

184
00:07:06,960 --> 00:07:08,800
that banks pay each other overnight.

185
00:07:08,820 --> 00:07:10,750
In euros, it's called €STR.

186
00:07:11,930 --> 00:07:14,870
Their value climbs slowly, almost every day.

187
00:07:14,890 --> 00:07:16,160
It's the closest thing

188
00:07:16,180 --> 00:07:18,430
to a savings account in ETF form.

189
00:07:19,600 --> 00:07:21,170
But it's not a bank account.

190
00:07:21,190 --> 00:07:22,930
There's no deposit guarantee.

191
00:07:24,330 --> 00:07:26,670
When rates are low, they earn almost nothing.

192
00:07:26,690 --> 00:07:28,140
When rates were negative,

193
00:07:28,160 --> 00:07:29,230
they even lost a little.

194
00:07:29,250 --> 00:07:31,210
When rates rise, they earn more.

195
00:07:33,230 --> 00:07:33,930
Box 9.

196
00:07:33,950 --> 00:07:34,910
Commodities.

197
00:07:35,570 --> 00:07:37,080
Gold is the classic.

198
00:07:37,100 --> 00:07:37,330
But in Europe,

199
00:07:37,350 --> 00:07:39,400
a regular fund isn't allowed

200
00:07:39,420 --> 00:07:41,070
to hold just one commodity.

201
00:07:42,230 --> 00:07:44,230
So gold trades as an ETC,

202
00:07:44,250 --> 00:07:46,400
an exchange-traded commodity.

203
00:07:46,420 --> 00:07:48,000
Often, each share is backed

204
00:07:48,020 --> 00:07:49,690
by real gold in a vault.

205
00:07:51,130 --> 00:07:53,280
Gold has long dry spells too.

206
00:07:53,300 --> 00:07:55,550
In dollars, it peaked in 2011,

207
00:07:55,570 --> 00:07:57,650
and only got back there in 2020.

208
00:07:58,970 --> 00:08:02,240
Other commodities, like oil, often use futures contracts.

209
00:08:02,260 --> 00:08:04,230
Their returns can drift away from

210
00:08:04,250 --> 00:08:06,030
the price you see on the news.

211
00:08:07,370 --> 00:08:10,480
And commodities pay no interest and no dividends.

212
00:08:10,500 --> 00:08:12,440
You only gain if the price goes up.

213
00:08:14,200 --> 00:08:14,760
Box 10.

214
00:08:14,780 --> 00:08:16,380
Leveraged and inverse.

215
00:08:17,130 --> 00:08:18,880
Leveraged products promise two

216
00:08:18,900 --> 00:08:19,740
or three times

217
00:08:19,760 --> 00:08:21,240
the daily move of an index.

218
00:08:21,260 --> 00:08:22,370
Inverse ones go up

219
00:08:22,390 --> 00:08:23,870
when the market goes down.

220
00:08:25,130 --> 00:08:26,480
The key word is daily.

221
00:08:26,500 --> 00:08:28,280
They reset every single day.

222
00:08:29,900 --> 00:08:31,840
If an index rises 10%,

223
00:08:31,860 --> 00:08:34,740
then falls 10%, it's down 1%.

224
00:08:34,760 --> 00:08:37,890
A two times leveraged product is down 4%.

225
00:08:39,270 --> 00:08:41,880
This loss is often called volatility decay.

226
00:08:41,900 --> 00:08:43,440
The more the market zigzags,

227
00:08:43,460 --> 00:08:44,580
the bigger it gets.

228
00:08:45,730 --> 00:08:46,840
In a choppy market,

229
00:08:46,860 --> 00:08:48,250
they can lose money even

230
00:08:48,270 --> 00:08:49,850
if the index ends flat.

231
00:08:49,870 --> 00:08:52,660
They're built for short-term traders, not for holding.

232
00:08:54,570 --> 00:08:55,460
Box 11.

233
00:08:55,480 --> 00:08:56,710
Active ETFs.

234
00:08:57,330 --> 00:08:58,630
Active ETFs have

235
00:08:58,650 --> 00:09:00,340
a manager who picks the stocks,

236
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:02,260
inside an ETF wrapper.

237
00:09:03,500 --> 00:09:04,950
They usually cost more,

238
00:09:04,970 --> 00:09:06,960
and the manager has to beat the market

239
00:09:06,980 --> 00:09:07,940
after those fees.

240
00:09:09,170 --> 00:09:11,910
According to S&P's long-running research,

241
00:09:11,930 --> 00:09:13,550
most active funds lag their

242
00:09:13,570 --> 00:09:15,020
index over the long run.

243
00:09:16,470 --> 00:09:18,170
Some managers do beat it.

244
00:09:18,190 --> 00:09:19,440
The hard part is knowing

245
00:09:19,460 --> 00:09:20,780
which ones, in advance.

246
00:09:22,430 --> 00:09:23,330
Box 12.

247
00:09:23,350 --> 00:09:24,590
Crypto ETPs.

248
00:09:25,300 --> 00:09:26,420
The last box isn't

249
00:09:26,440 --> 00:09:28,120
technically an ETF at all.

250
00:09:29,400 --> 00:09:30,750
In Europe, a regular

251
00:09:30,770 --> 00:09:32,980
ETF can't hold bitcoin directly.

252
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,410
So crypto products are ETPs,

253
00:09:35,430 --> 00:09:38,180
debt-like notes, often backed by real coins.

254
00:09:39,400 --> 00:09:41,490
America approved its first spot

255
00:09:41,510 --> 00:09:44,170
bitcoin ETFs in January 2024.

256
00:09:44,190 --> 00:09:46,670
In Europe, they're still ETPs.

257
00:09:48,100 --> 00:09:49,050
You get the same

258
00:09:49,070 --> 00:09:50,860
wild price swings as crypto,

259
00:09:50,880 --> 00:09:52,320
plus the risk of the company

260
00:09:52,340 --> 00:09:53,540
that issues the note.

261
00:09:54,970 --> 00:09:56,330
If you want the full story,

262
00:09:56,350 --> 00:09:58,830
our last video explains every crypto,

263
00:09:58,850 --> 00:09:59,920
box by box.

264
00:10:01,070 --> 00:10:03,780
Before you buy any ETF, read its name.

265
00:10:03,800 --> 00:10:05,380
It tells you the provider,

266
00:10:05,400 --> 00:10:06,160
the index, the EU rules,

267
00:10:06,180 --> 00:10:08,220
the fund currency,

268
00:10:08,240 --> 00:10:09,920
and what happens to the dividends.

269
00:10:11,330 --> 00:10:12,770
Then check three things.

270
00:10:12,790 --> 00:10:14,080
The yearly fee, called TER.

271
00:10:14,100 --> 00:10:16,160
The size of the fund.

272
00:10:16,180 --> 00:10:18,280
And how closely it follows its index.

273
00:10:19,470 --> 00:10:21,200
Those are the twelve boxes.

274
00:10:21,220 --> 00:10:22,780
Almost every ETF you'll see

275
00:10:22,800 --> 00:10:24,090
fits into one of them.

276
00:10:25,570 --> 00:10:27,820
Remember, ETFs can lose value,

277
00:10:27,840 --> 00:10:30,050
and nothing here is a recommendation.

278
00:10:30,070 --> 00:10:32,130
This is not financial advice.

279
00:10:33,570 --> 00:10:35,110
Twelve boxes, eleven minutes.

280
00:10:35,130 --> 00:10:36,910
Now, when you see a new ETF,

281
00:10:36,930 --> 00:10:38,690
you'll know which box it's in,

282
00:10:38,710 --> 00:10:39,650
and what to ask.

283
00:10:40,900 --> 00:10:42,600
If this helped, subscribe.

284
00:10:42,620 --> 00:10:43,880
